{"causes":[
{"cause":"A new HTML page shipped with no-referrer again, and the enumerated header rail never saw it. The policy lives in THREE separately hand-built header blocks (session.ts:645 html(), oauth.ts:621 htmlOut(), admin.ts:231 htmlOut()), and criterion 1's test 'asserts the header on a live GET of each page' — a hand-enumerated page list. Six months of admin growth (admin.ts already has ~12 postGate forms) added a page via a copied header block or a fourth helper; its form POSTs sent Origin: null; every enumerated page stayed green. This is H10's self-referential-list disguise: the rail's coverage narrows in lockstep with what the author remembered to list, and there is no floor tying the list to the set of text/html emitters in src/server.","earliest_signal":"grep -c 'no-referrer' src/server/*.ts disagrees with the count of sites criterion 2 permits (JSON helpers + the authorize 302 + the admin 303 seeOther at admin.ts:1018 — which criterion 2 does not even name); or referrer-policy.test.ts's page list is shorter than the set of writeHead sites sending text/html.","prevention":"Flip the three shared helpers, not eight call sites, and add a coverage rail: every `text/html` writeHead site in src/server is either asserted same-origin or in a hand-written no-referrer allowlist with a floor count, so adding a page without classifying it goes red.","interrogation_question":"Criterion 1 lists pages by hand — what makes the list complete, and what turns red when the NEXT HTML page is added with the old header block?"},
{"cause":"The spec's own two statements of scope disagree, and the store's OTHER HTML documents fell through the gap. The decision says 'Every HTML document this store serves sends referrer-policy: same-origin'; criterion 1 narrows to 'every HTML document that hosts a form'. But the front-door greeting (http.ts:999) sends NO referrer-policy at all, and the render-worker serves rendered artifact pages (render-worker.ts, artifact-page.ts) whose script intercepts <form> submits into fetch POSTs against store doors (artifact-page.ts:426-450). A fetch inherits its document's referrer policy, and a no-referrer document serializes the request origin as null on POST — the exact T143 mechanism, one surface over. When artifact pages later gained the 'standard' security headers, every artifact-hosted write died against fromThisPage while all three enumerated stories stayed green.","earliest_signal":"An artifact page's form 'looks like it worked and did nothing' (artifact-page.ts:439's own words) in a real browser while test/server/artifact-write.test.ts — hand-built headers — stays green.","prevention":"Resolve the decision-vs-criterion mismatch in P1: enumerate ALL HTML the store serves (greeting, rendered artifact pages, admin, login, consent) and state each one's policy explicitly; extend the CDP rail with one artifact-page form submit.","interrogation_question":"The decision text says 'every HTML document' and criterion 1 says 'every HTML document that hosts a form' — which one is the promise, and what policy do the render-worker's artifact pages get, whose forms POST via fetch under the same Origin-serialization rule?"},
{"cause":"The repair was proven in exactly one browser, and the Origin-absent fallback was never exercised by any real one. fromThisPage's second arm (session.ts:450) requires sec-fetch-site: same-origin when Origin is missing — and Safari before 16.4 sends no Sec-Fetch-Site, while several Safari and older-Firefox versions omit Origin on same-origin form POSTs. Such a browser hits `{}` — the first refusing shape in the frozen rail's own list (login-csrf.test.ts). Three months in, every Safari-15 user got the same universal 403 Myk got in Chrome, discovered the same way: by a human, because the only browser rail pins Chrome. T143's lesson was 'no rail drives a browser'; the fix installed a rail that drives ONE browser and let 'works in a real browser' quietly mean 'works in Chrome'.","earliest_signal":"A support report of the T143 symptom from a non-Chrome browser; or simply reading session.ts:450 against caniuse Sec-Fetch-Site data.","prevention":"Per the P3 name-the-gap rule, door-smoke.test.ts states in its header which browsers it does NOT cover and which header combinations each would send; add one hand-built-header case per known browser shape (Origin absent + Sec-Fetch-Site absent) and DECIDE whether that refusal is intended, in the spec, not by omission.","interrogation_question":"Which real browsers send neither Origin nor Sec-Fetch-Site on a same-origin form POST, and is refusing them a decision this spec is making on purpose?"},
{"cause":"The CDP rail is structurally blind to the deployment shape that failed: ownOrigins is exactly the settled public URL's origin, loopback-widened (session.ts:399-430), and the browser rail necessarily runs against http://127.0.0.1 — where everything matches by construction. Production is the rehearsed primary-store shape: TLS funnel on 8443 in front of the store. Booted with an http:// or wrong-port --public-url, the browser sends Origin: https://store.example while ownOrigins holds the internal spelling; every POST refuses — the identical universal-403 symptom T143's own ticket predicted ('It fails behind a TLS terminator too'), with every rail green because the rail's Origin equals the rail's public-url by construction. The repair fixed the header and shipped no rail for the origin-agreement it newly depends on.","earliest_signal":"First login attempt through the 8443 funnel after a deploy; earlier: the boot faults for unparseable/unroutable public URLs (session.ts:408,415) name this class but nothing faults on scheme/port disagreement between --public-url and the observed Origin.","prevention":"One story rail behind a TLS-terminating proxy fixture (the funnel recipe already exists in memory), or a loud fault: on the first refused POST whose Origin differs from ownOrigins only in scheme/port, say '--public-url disagrees with the address the browser used' — turning a silent universal 403 into a one-line diagnosis.","interrogation_question":"When this store sits behind the 8443 funnel, what proves the browser's Origin and ownOrigins agree — and what does the operator see when they do not?"},
{"cause":"Criterion 6 — the rail FAILS when Chrome is absent, never skips — collided with the green-bar economy and lost. npm run check is the P4 bar, the self-merge condition, and hollow-test's required-green baseline; on any box without Chrome (CI containers, a fresh runner image, the plugin-less WSL box) the whole bar went red by design. Add ordinary CDP flakiness (headless startup under load, sandbox flags in containers, port contention with parallel vitest workers) and the flaky-test standing rule was invoked against the rail itself: it became `it.skip` with a ticket id, or gated behind an env var 'temporarily'. The only end-to-end rail went dark, and the next header/CSP regression shipped green — T143's exact mechanism, restored by the remedy's own operational cost.","earliest_signal":"The first `npm run check` on a Chrome-less machine, or the first re-run-to-green of door-smoke.test.ts in CI.","prevention":"Decide Chrome provisioning in THIS PR, not later: pin an install step in .github/workflows/ci.yml, document LOAM_CHROME for the WSL box, give the rail CDP-scale timeouts and its own serial vitest project so parallel workers never share the browser or the port.","interrogation_question":"On which machines will npm run check now be red because Chrome is absent, and who installs Chrome where before this merges?"},
{"cause":"same-origin re-opened a leak no-referrer had closed, on the one page whose URL is a credential. /oauth/authorize's consent document carries client_id, state, code_challenge, and redirect_uri in its query. Under same-origin, that full URL becomes the Referer on every same-origin navigation and subresource from it — into the store's own request logs, and into document.referrer of the next same-origin page visited, including rendered artifact pages running third-party bundle code. A policy of `origin` would have fixed the POST identically (the null-Origin serialization clause applies only to no-referrer) while leaking scheme://host instead of the live PKCE challenge and state. The spec chose the broadest fixing policy for every page uniformly and never weighed per-page what each URL contains.","earliest_signal":"document.referrer non-empty, containing code_challenge and state, inside any page loaded after consent — checkable in the story-2 CDP run today.","prevention":"Pick the policy per page in P1: `origin` (or keep the fix but strip OAuth params into POST-carried state) for the consent document; assert in the CDP rail that no subsequent same-origin document can read the authorize query via document.referrer.","interrogation_question":"The consent page's URL carries code_challenge and state — what may now legitimately read that URL as a Referer, and why is `origin` (which also un-nulls the POST) not the chosen policy there?"},
{"cause":"The privacy claim guarding the foreign redirect_uri has no rail, and the mechanism the spec credits is not the one doing the work. Criterion 2's rationale says the authorize 302 'must keep the strictest policy so nothing leaks toward a foreign redirect_uri' — but the Referer sent to the redirect_uri host is governed by the consent DOCUMENT's policy (now weakened to same-origin, which omits Referer cross-origin) plus Chrome's honoring of Referrer-Policy on redirect responses. That protection held by two implementation details, and no test anywhere asserts what actually arrives at the redirect_uri end — story 2's rail checks only that the code lands in the query. A later 'harmless' relaxation of the html helper (e.g. to strict-origin-when-cross-origin, the browser default) would have started announcing the store's origin — and with it the fact of an OAuth grant — to every connector, under a fully green suite.","earliest_signal":"The story-2 CDP rail already terminates at a local redirect_uri listener; printing that request's headers shows what the spec never asserted.","prevention":"One added assertion in the existing story-2 rail: the request landing at the registered redirect_uri carries no Referer header. It is a five-line, two-sided close on the exact claim criterion 2 makes in prose.","interrogation_question":"What test observes the request that arrives AT the redirect_uri, and what exactly does it assert about its Referer?"}
]}
